sentences.t:
Tráyovos [treínta] palafrés, estos bien adobados,
e treínta cavallos corredores, estos bien enssellados;
tomad aquesto, e beso vuestras manos.”

sentences.l:
I-bring-you [thirty] palfreys, these well fitted-out,
and thirty horses swift, these well saddled;
take this, and I-kiss your hands.”

sentences.t:
Mio Çid R[o]y Diaz, mucho me avedes ondrado,
de vos bien so servido, e tengon por pagado;
aun bivo se[d]iendo, de mí ayades algo!

sentences.l:
My Çid R[o]y Diaz, much me you-have honoured,
by you well I-am served, and I-hold-myself for satisfied;
still living being, from me may-you-have something!

sentences.t:
Esto plogo al rey, e a todos los soltó;
la conpaña del Çid creçe, e la del rey mengó,
grandes son las yentes que van con el Canpeador.

sentences.l:
This pleased to-the king, and all them he-released;
the company of-the Çid grows, and that of-the king dwindled,
great are the people that go with the Campeador.

sentences.t:
Todos essa noch f[o]ron a sus posadas,
mio Çid el Campeador al alcáç[er] entrava;
reçibiólo doña Ximena e sus fijas amas:

sentences.l:
All that night w[e]nt to their lodgings,
my Çid the Campeador into-the citadel was-entering;
received-him doña Ximena and her daughters both:

sentences.t:
—“Grado al Criador, vengo, mugier ondrada!
yernos vos adugo de que avremos ondrança;
gradídmelo, mis fijas, ca bien vos he casadas!”

sentences.l:
—“Thanks to-the Creator, I-come, wife honoured!
sons-in-law to-you I-bring of whom we-shall-have honour;
thank-me-for-it, my daughters, for well you I-have married!”
